    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d105">

                <pb facs="0287" n="257" xml:id="pg257"/>

                <head>105. Telegram From the Embassy in France to the Department of State<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d105-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Paris</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1965-04-29T18:00:00+00:00">April 29, 1965, 1800Z</date>.<anchor
                            n="2" xml:id="d105-f2"/>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p>6136. When Lucet briefed DCM on Gromyko communiqué,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d105-f3"
                    /> he made declaration of profound unhappiness over Washington Ambassadorial
                    Group problem on declaration. He said French felt they had met our point after
                    misunderstanding when French thought matter had been settled proved erroneous.
                    French now felt, he continued, that we were trying to impose something. He
                    stressed French were acting in good faith and not trying undermine Four-Power
                    responsibility for Germany. He referred again to French action in adding
                    reference to Geneva declaration of 1955<anchor n="4" xml:id="d105-f4"/> on this
                    point. He expressed question as to whether US was trying to maneuver so that it
                    could pin responsibility for absence of agreed declaration on French especially
                    as time growing so short.</p>
                <p>British sources here indicate they have information to effect Foreign Office is
                    in bind because present French language drafted by de Gaulle who instructed Quai
                    to insist on its inclusion.</p>
                <p>Regular Franco-German monthly meeting at Lucet-Meyer-Lindberg level will take
                    place May 3 and French plan raise matter urgently with Germans then if it is not
                    settled in interim.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#bold">Bohlen</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d105-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d105-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files,POL 32–4 GER. Secret. Repeated to
                    Bonn, London, and Moscow. A notation on the source text reads: “Handled Limdis
                    per S/S-O.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d105-f2" n="2" xml:id="d105-f2-content">
                <p>The Z refers to Greenwich Mean Time.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d105-f3" n="3" xml:id="d105-f3-content">
                <p>For text, see <hi rendition="#italic">American Foreign Policy: Current
                        Documents</hi>, 1965, pp. 522–523.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d105-f4" n="4" xml:id="d105-f4-content">
                <p>For text of the Four-Power declaration on Germany, November 16, 1955, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">American Foreign Policy, 1950–1955: Basic
                    Documents</hi>, vol. I, pp. 1926–1927.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
